---
/**
 * foodcraft / FaqBN — "FAQ" (Panday Coffee Works / BN).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's FAQ element — one uppercase Archivo headline over a RULED
 * TWO-UP TABLE of question-and-answer cells, hung on the page spine at index 10.
 * NO ACCORDION: the design draws every answer open. On a six-seat coffee bar the
 * answers ARE the practicalities, and someone deciding whether to make the trip
 * should not have to click to find out.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * element carrying data-component="FAQ" in "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html",
 * mapped hex → wrapper token (R1 — not one literal hex appears in this file):
 *   section    id="faq", border-bottom 1px #17181A, grid minmax(0,52px)
 *              minmax(0,1fr) ......... the wrapper's .sec, used and NOT rebuilt
 *   rail       border-right 1px #C8C7C2, flex-end/centre, padding 20px 0 ..
 *              .rail; its rotated 10px/.3em mono label in #A8351B .... .rail-t
 *   body cell  padding 48px 34px ......... .sec-body, with only the VERTICAL
 *              48px restated (the wrapper's clamp caps at 44px) — see the style
 *              block. The 34px sides stay the wrapper's clamp so this section's
 *              text edge is flush with its neighbours'.
 *   headline   800 clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) / line-height 1 / -.03em / uppercase,
 *              margin 0 0 30px, #17181A ..................... .h2 + var(--ink)
 *   table      grid repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)), border-top 1px #17181A
 *              (--rule-strong, the page's full-strength rule) and border-left
 *              1px #C8C7C2 (--line, the interior grey). The two weights are the
 *              design's own distinction and are NOT collapsed to one.
 *   cell       padding 24px 26px, border-right + border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2
 *   numeral    mono 10px / .14em / #A8351B, margin-bottom 10px ...... --accent
 *   question   700 18px / -.01em, margin 0 0 8px, #17181A ........... --ink
 *   answer     14.5px / 1.8, margin 0, #3C3E42 ..................... --ink-2
 *              (#3C3E42 IS --ink-2 exactly; it is not --ink-soft #5A5C60, and
 *              rounding the two together would lighten the design's body copy.)
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under
 * section id 'faq'. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook no sidebar
 * input owns, so nothing outside this list is bound:
 *   faq.headline         — the h2, fed with set:html so an owner can place this
 *                          template's SECOND VOICE anywhere inside their own
 *                          sentence. That voice is a COLOUR CHANGE to rust, not
 *                          an italic — Archivo has no italic worth using and the
 *                          design never asks for one. The rule that paints it,
 *                          :where(.h2 em), is GLOBAL in PageBN and is therefore
 *                          NOT restated here: a scoped rule cannot reach markup
 *                          made by set:html (R3), and the wrapper's colour is
 *                          already correct on this paper ground.
 *   faq.items.${i}.q     — the question
 *   faq.items.${i}.a     — the answer, also set:html so an owner can emphasise,
 *                          bold or link inside their own sentence
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying no data-field and never stored:
 *   · the rail label "10 / Notes" — the spine's index for this section's place
 *     in PageBN's render order (Hero 00, About 01, Services 02, Why 03,
 *     Gallery 04, How 05, Testimonials 06, Credentials 07, Location 08,
 *     Area 09, FAQ 10, CtaBand 11 — the design's own numbering, unchanged).
 *     Decoration, aria-hidden.
 *   · the cell numeral "N01", "N02" … — the design's index glyph for this
 *     table, "N" for the Notes the rail names. Built from the DISPLAY position
 *     (R6), never the original index, so dropping a blank row cannot print
 *     N01, N03, N04. Decoration, aria-hidden.
 *   · the reveal wrappers and their data-delay step.
 *
 * R2 — NOT ONE WORD IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE, and on an FAQ that rule is
 * load-bearing beyond the usual: an answer is a promise about a business this
 * template has never visited. So there is no opening or closing time, no
 * reservation policy, no wifi or power-outlet claim, no delivery promise, no
 * card-versus-cash line, no "we reply within…", and no example question. The
 * design's own demo strings ("Asked at the bar" and its six sample rows, right
 * down to "six seats and no power outlets") are the DESIGNER filling in their
 * own template; they are not this file's to type.
 *
 * R5 — THE SECTION GATE COUNTS THE ROWS, never the tag and never the headline.
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills faq.tag and faq.headline on EVERY site
 * while emitting items only when the submission actually answered something. A
 * gate on headline would ship a 50px headline standing over an empty ruled
 * table on any shop whose owner typed no questions. With no filled row the whole
 * <section> is omitted — no rail, no headline, no table, no padding and no rule.
 *
 * R6 — blank rows are dropped, but every kept row carries its ORIGINAL index
 * into data-field, so deleting question 2 can never misdirect an edit into
 * question 3. The numeral is the one thing keyed to the display position,
 * because it is decoration rather than a hook.
 *
 * ROW GUARDS: a cell is kept when EITHER field has content, and the two content
 * tags are guarded independently. Silently vanishing an answer an owner typed is
 * a worse failure than printing a lone paragraph in a ruled cell. Every guard
 * sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a half-filled row prints only what was actually
 * typed — never an empty <h3> holding open 8px of margin over a blank line box.
 * Fields are TRIMMED before they are tested, because a value holding only a
 * space is truthy and would print exactly that empty element.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of it reads as an oversight:
 *   · faq.tag. The design gives this section NO eyebrow — the headline sits
 *     straight on the ruled table, and .kick appears nowhere in the element.
 *     derive-content-defaults emits a tag on every site, so rendering it would
 *     print a mark the design does not have on essentially every build. The hook
 *     is left to the templates whose design draws one.
 *   · NO STAR ROW and no rating ornament of any kind. Five stars printed over a
 *     customer who gave no rating is a fabricated claim; that treatment belongs
 *     to TESTIMONIALS, where it is drawn only from a numeric
 *     testimonials.items.N.rating, clamped 1–5, aria-hidden, with the number
 *     itself in the accessible label. An FAQ row carries no rating field to draw
 *     from, so there is nothing here to draw — not even the plain rule that
 *     stands in for a missing one.
 *   · no <details>/<summary> accordion. The design shows every answer open.
 *   · no button and no link of any kind. The design gives the FAQ no action; the
 *     contact routes live in the header, the CTA band and the footer.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER, and that is a decision rather than an
 *     omission (R7). The helper exists to rewrite an owner's href onto the ids
 *     this page really has — for BN that set is `menu about gallery visit faq
 *     main`, with location/contact → visit, services/rooms → menu, host/why →
 *     about, work → gallery, book → main, and tel: / mailto: / http passing
 *     through untouched. This section emits NO href at all: no button, no link,
 *     no data-href-field. A remap helper with nothing to remap is dead code a
 *     later reader would have to prove unused, so it is not carried. The one
 *     anchor this file owns is its own id="faq" — already in that set, and in
 *     the PAGE_ANCHORS both HeaderBN and FooterBN declare, so an owner's own
 *     link to it passes their remap untouched.
 *   · no .plate, no role="img", no image. There is no upload behind this band,
 *     so there is nothing to name and nothing to hide (R9). Nothing in this file
 *     emits a conditional accessibility attribute, so there is no way for a
 *     literal aria-hidden to be duplicated beside a conditional one.
 *   · no data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one where two content
 *     prefixes share a <section>. This band is faq.* alone, so the Blocks toggle
 *     resolves to it by hook weight already.
 *   · NO FILLER CELL when the row count is ODD. The table's rules are drawn by
 *     the cells themselves, so five questions leave the second half of the last
 *     row open. That is honest: a phantom sixth cell would rule a hairline under
 *     a row that is not there, which is exactly what R2 forbids.
 *
 * THE QUESTION IS NOT UPPERCASED, and that is deliberate. PageBN sets
 * `text-transform: uppercase` on :where(h1,h2,h3,h4) because the DISPLAY voice
 * of this template is caps; the design's FAQ h3 declares no text-transform and
 * the design's own stylesheet sets none, so a question renders in the case the
 * owner typed it. `text-transform: none` is restated below to hold the design,
 * not to fight the wrapper.
 *
 * VERIFIED CONTRAST on the paper ground — COMPUTED (sRGB relative luminance,
 * WCAG 2.x) from the hexes PageBN declares, not estimated:
 *   question --ink #17181A on --paper #EFEEEA .......... 15.30
 *   answer   --ink-2 #3C3E42 on --paper ................. 9.23
 *   numeral  --accent #A8351B on --paper ................ 5.67
 *   an owner's link inside an answer, --accent on --paper  5.67
 * Nothing in this section is below AA; the tightest pair is 5.67. The hairlines
 * (--line, 1.46 on paper) are non-text structure and are not held to that bar,
 * which is why the design also rules the table's TOP in --rule-strong.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style> block, no is:global. Both the headline and the answers
 * arrive through set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach inside them: the
 * headline's rust second voice is painted by PageBN's global :where(.h2 em), and
 * an <em>, <strong> or <a> inside an answer is reached from here via :global().
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN passes it to every section uniformly.
// This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


/* Coerce at EVERY read. A string passes; a finite number passes as its digits;
   null, undefined, NaN, an array and an object are not content and become ''.
   Nothing downstream calls a string method on raw input, so no shape from the
   pipeline can fail the BUILD — and an object reaching set:html would otherwise
   print the literal "[object Object]" onto a customer's page. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const faq = content.faq || {};
const headline = str(faq.headline).trim();

/* The schema declares 'faq.items' with fallbackPaths ['faq'], so the stored
   value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a bare array.
   Both shapes are read. */
type Item = { q?: unknown; a?: unknown; question?: unknown; answer?: unknown };
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(faq.items)
  ? faq.items
  : (Array.isArray(faq) ? (faq as Item[]) : []);

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index (R6), then drop the rows
   that cannot render an honest cell. The row itself is coerced before any field
   is read: a stored list can carry a null slot left by an older editor, and a
   bare string row is the question the owner typed rather than a row to discard.
   Legacy submissions spell the pair {question, answer}; the schema and every
   current writer spell {q, a}. Both are read, but the data-field hook always
   spells q / a — that is the path the sidebar input owns. */
const rows = items
  .map((raw, i: number) => {
    const it: Item = raw && typeof raw === 'object' ? (raw as Item) : ({ q: raw } as Item);
    return {
      i,
      q: (str(it.q) || str(it.question)).trim(),
      a: (str(it.a) || str(it.answer)).trim(),
    };
  })
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.q || r.a));

/* The spine's index for this section's place in the render order. Decoration,
   never stored, never editable — see DERIVED above. */
const RAIL_INDEX = 'Notes';

/* The design's cell glyph: "N" for Notes, then a zero-padded DISPLAY position.
   Never the original index — this numeral is decoration and must not count
   N01, N03, N04 after a blank row is dropped. */
const numeral = (n: number): string => 'N' + (n < 10 ? '0' + String(n) : String(n));
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sec pcwq" id="faq">
    {/* THE SPINE. .sec, .rail and .rail-t are PageBN's primitives, worn rather
        than rebuilt, so this section cannot drift from its neighbours. */}
    <div class="rail">
      <span class="rail-t" aria-hidden="true">{RAIL_INDEX}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body pcwq-body">
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 pcwq-h2 reveal" data-field="faq.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}

      <div class="pcwq-grid">
        {rows.map((row, d: number) => (
          /* `d` is the DISPLAY position and `row.i` is the ORIGINAL one. Only
             the numeral and the reveal step are keyed to `d`. */
          <div class="pcwq-cell reveal" data-delay={d > 0 ? String(Math.min(d, 3)) : undefined}>
            <div class="pcwq-n" aria-hidden="true">{numeral(d + 1)}</div>
            {row.q && <h3 class="pcwq-q" data-field={`faq.items.${row.i}.q`}>{row.q}</h3>}
            {row.a && <p class="pcwq-a" data-field={`faq.items.${row.i}.a`} set:html={row.a}></p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* ── THE CONTENT CELL. The wrapper's .sec-body already supplies min-width:0
        and the horizontal clamp(20px,3vw,34px) that keeps every section's text
        edge on the same line; only the VERTICAL padding is this file's, because
        the design writes 48px here against the primitive's 44px/40px.

        LADDER, traced across breakpoints so it can only ever SHRINK as the
        viewport narrows (R8):
          1091px+ .. 48px (cap)  ·  900px .. 39.6px  ·  760px .. 33.4px
          682px and below .. 30px (floor), flat from there
        No media query in this file restates it, so that ramp is the whole of
        it. */
  .pcwq-body {
    padding-top: clamp(30px, 4.4vw, 48px);
    padding-bottom: clamp(30px, 4.4vw, 48px);
  }

  /* ── THE HEADLINE. Face, weight, tracking, caps and colour are already the
        .h2 primitive's; only the three divergences are written. The design
        states NO max-width on this h2 and the wrapper caps .h2 at 20ch, so the
        cap is removed rather than silently halving the design's measure — this
        headline sits over a full-width table, not beside a column.

        THE CLAMP LADDER. Astro scopes this rule to `.pcwq-h2[data-astro-cid-…]`
        — (0,2,0) — against the wrapper's `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.h2)` at
        (0,1,1), so it wins at EVERY width, its 760px pass included, and the
        whole ramp is this file's to keep monotonic:
          1389px+ .. 50px (cap)  ·  1160px .. 41.8px  ·  900px .. 32.4px
          778px and below .. 28px (floor), flat from there
        Nothing below re-states the size — see the note in the 520 block — so
        the type only ever DECREASES as the viewport narrows and never goes under
        the design's own floor. */
  .pcwq-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0 0 30px;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE RULED TABLE, drawn with BORDERS rather than gaps. The design rules
        the TOP in full-strength ink and the LEFT in interior grey — two
        different weights, deliberately, because the top rule belongs to the
        page's spine and the left one to the table's own interior. Collapsing
        them to one would flatten that distinction.
        minmax(0,1fr) rather than 1fr so one long unbroken word in an answer
        cannot widen its own track and push the page sideways. */
  .pcwq-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Design: padding 24px 26px, border-right + border-bottom. min-width:0 as
        well as the minmax() above — a grid item's default min-width is auto, so
        one unbroken word would still widen its track. */
  .pcwq-cell {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 24px 26px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* ── THE INDEX GLYPH. Design: mono 10px / .14em / rust, margin-bottom 10px,
        and NO font-weight and NO line-height declared. The design's own body
        sets neither, so both compute from the initial value; PageBN's body sets
        line-height 1.62, which grows this 10px glyph's line box by ~3px (16.2px
        against the ~13px IBM Plex Mono renders at `normal`) and drops half of
        that — ~1.6px — under the glyph, on top of the 10px margin the design
        draws there. `normal` is restated to hold the drawn gap. The weight
        is stated as 400 for the same reason: the wrapper's .mono primitive is
        500 at .2em in --muted and is deliberately NOT worn here, because none of
        its three values is the one the design asks for.
        No overflow-wrap: the content is a derived three-character glyph. */
  .pcwq-n {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: normal;
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  /* ── THE QUESTION. The face, the colour (--ink) and the margin reset are the
        wrapper's shared h1–h4 rule already. Restated here are the four values
        that DIVERGE from it: the WEIGHT (800 there), the TRACKING (-.03em
        there), the LEADING (.98 there, but the design states none on this h3 and
        its stylesheet sets none, so it renders `normal` — guessing a number
        would be a divergence, and .98 is a VISIBLE one the moment an owner's
        question runs longer than the designer's and wraps), and the CASE. On
        the case, see the note in the frontmatter: the display voice of this
        template is uppercase, this h3 is not. */
  .pcwq-q {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 18px;
    letter-spacing: -.01em;
    line-height: normal;
    text-transform: none;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE ANSWER. Qualified with the cell class so it beats the wrapper's
        bare-element paragraph reset, `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}`, and the
        design's zero margin actually lands. Deliberately NOT the .body
        primitive: .body is a clamped 14–15px at 1.62, and the design pins this
        run at a flat 14.5px at 1.8. */
  .pcwq-cell p.pcwq-a {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The answer arrives through set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach inside it
     (R3). An owner's emphasis takes this template's SECOND VOICE — a colour
     change to rust, font-style explicitly normal, because Archivo has no italic
     worth using and the design never asks for one. --accent is 5.67:1 on paper,
     which clears AA for this size. */
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(em),
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(i) {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(strong),
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(b) {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* An owner's link keeps a PERMANENT rule under it, unlike the wrapper's .linkc
     which underlines on hover only. That is not a style choice: an <em> in this
     band is already rust, so colour alone could not tell a link from emphasis,
     and the design's own global anchor rule paints links rust with no underline
     at all. The rest state is a .45 alpha off --accent-rgb so the rule reads as
     an underline rather than a border; hover takes it to full strength. The text
     colour does not move, so it cannot fail a contrast check halfway through an
     interaction. */
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(a) {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(var(--accent-rgb), .45);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcwq-cell .pcwq-a :global(a:hover) {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom-color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* ── 900 · this section's OWN grid, which is the part R8 leaves to this file.
        Two tracks are the design. Measured at the 900px edge where this query
        engages: 900 less the 52px rail is 848, less the body's 2×27px (3vw at
        900) is 794, less the grid's 1px left rule is 793 — so each track is
        ~396px, and once the cell's 26+26px padding and its 1px right rule come
        off, ~343px of text. At 18px that is roughly a 38-character measure for a
        question and ~42 for the answer under it, which wraps an ordinary
        question to two or three lines — so the table folds to a single column.
        Stacked, every cell keeps BOTH vertical rules: the grid's left and the
        cell's right together close the box, which is the design's treatment for
        this table.
        The answer takes a measure cap in exchange: at one column that same 793px
        track leaves ~740px of text — roughly 90 characters at 14.5px — just
        because its cell is now the whole band. Nothing else is touched here —
        in particular no font-size, because the base clamps are already handing
        over 32.4px and 39.6px at this width and any cap written below those
        numbers would make the headline and the padding JUMP as the viewport
        crossed 900px. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pcwq-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pcwq-cell p.pcwq-a { max-width: 68ch; }
  }

  /* ── 520 · nothing may push the page sideways. The single track left by the
        900 query is minmax(0,1fr), every cell is min-width:0 and every string in
        here breaks anywhere, so the remaining work is scale. EVERY value in
        this block is strictly BELOW the value it replaces, so nothing grows as
        the viewport narrows:
          cell padding   20px 18px  <  24px 26px
          question       17px       <  18px
          answer         14px       <  14.5px, and the 68ch cap comes off so an
                         answer is not set narrower than the phone reading it —
                         at 14px, 68ch is ~530px, while a 320px phone leaves only
                         ~242px of text here (320 less the body's 2×20px, less
                         the grid's 1px left rule, less the cell's 18+18px and
                         its 1px right rule; ~282px at 360px). The cap was never
                         the constraint at these widths, so releasing it widens
                         nothing.
        THE HEADLINE IS NOT RE-CLAMPED HERE, and neither is the body padding.
        Both reach their design floors at 778px and 682px and hold flat from
        there; those floors are the design's own answer for a narrow viewport,
        and going under them would be a divergence with nothing asking for it.
        Re-stating a clamp inside a max-width query is also the exact construct
        that shipped type which GREW as the viewport narrowed on two earlier
        audits. The numeral is left at 10px: it is the design's smallest type
        already. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .pcwq-cell { padding: 20px 18px; }
    .pcwq-q { font-size: 17px; }
    .pcwq-cell p.pcwq-a { font-size: 14px; max-width: none; }
  }
</style>
